#doc (Manual) "The Orlik–Solomon theorem from the axioms of cohomology" =>
%%%
shortTitle := "Orlik–Solomon from axioms"
authors := ["Adam Topaz", "Claude", "Codex"]
%%%

This book explains a formalization, in Lean and Mathlib, of the Orlik–Solomon structure theorem for the cohomology of complements of hyperplane arrangements, in affine space and in projective space.
Both theorems are proved, for every cohomology theory that satisfies the axioms.
For algebraic de Rham cohomology over a field of characteristic zero, which the library proves satisfies the axioms, they are also proved as theorems with no hypothetical cohomology theory in them, as explained below.
The book is written for a working mathematician who wants to know what exactly is proved, from what, and how.
No knowledge of Lean is needed: the prose stands on its own, and the Lean statements are shown so that you can compare them with the mathematics.
For the public statement in one auditable file, start with {ref "main-theorem"}[The main theorem].
That chapter quotes its definitions and statements using Mathlib objects, and explains the hypothesis of a theory and the verified affine de Rham example.

{statusSummary}

*The setting.*
Fix a field $`k` and a finite set $`\mathcal{A}` of distinct hyperplanes defined over $`k` in an affine space of dimension $`n`, and let $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` be the complement of their union.
The classical theorem of Orlik and Solomon presents the cohomology of the complement of a complex arrangement by generators and relations that can be read off from the incidences of the hyperplanes.
Here the cohomology theory is not fixed.
It is any theory with bigraded groups $`H^{p,w}`, where $`p` is the cohomological degree and $`w` is the Tate twist, that satisfies the axioms of {ref "axioms"}[the cohomology axioms chapter].
Write
$$`B=\bigoplus_{w\in\mathbb{Z}}H^{0,w}(k),\qquad C(X)=\bigoplus_{(p,w)\in\mathbb{Z}^2}H^{p,w}(X)`
for the cohomology of the point and the total cohomology of $`X`, with coefficient ring $`R=B_0`.

The _Orlik–Solomon algebra_ $`\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})` is the quotient of the exterior algebra over $`B` on generators $`e_H`, one for each hyperplane, by a two-sided ideal.
For an ordered list $`I=(H_1,\ldots,H_s)` of distinct hyperplanes, put $`e_I=e_{H_1}\cdots e_{H_s}` and
$$`\partial e_I=\sum_{j=1}^s(-1)^{j-1}e_{H_1}\cdots\widehat{e_{H_j}}\cdots e_{H_s}.`
The ideal is generated by $`e_I` when $`\bigcap I` is empty, and by $`\partial e_I` when $`\bigcap I` is nonempty of codimension less than $`s`.
An equation $`f_H` of a hyperplane is invertible on $`U_{\mathcal{A}}`, so it has a logarithmic class $`[f_H]` of bidegree $`(1,1)`, which {ref "logarithms"}[is constructed from the axioms].

:::statement "Main theorem, affine case"
Let $`H^{\bullet,\bullet}` be a cohomology theory over $`k` that satisfies the axioms.
For every finite-dimensional affine space and every arrangement $`\mathcal{A}` in it:

 * *The total ring.* There is an isomorphism of $`B`-algebras
   $$`\Phi_{\mathcal{A}}:\operatorname{OS}_B(\mathcal{A})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C(U_{\mathcal{A}}),\qquad e_H\longmapsto[f_H],`
   which preserves the bigrading, with $`e_H` in bidegree $`(1,1)` and $`B_w` in bidegree $`(0,w)`. The formula holds for every equation $`f_H` of $`H`, so the map does not depend on the equations.
 * *The groups.* For all integers $`p\ge0` and $`w` there is a canonical isomorphism of $`R`-modules
   $$`H^{p,w}(U_{\mathcal{A}})\simeq\operatorname{OS}_R^p(\mathcal{A})\otimes_R B_{w-p},`
   and $`H^{p,w}(U_{\mathcal{A}})=0` for $`p<0` and for $`p>n`.
 * *The diagonal.* There is a canonical isomorphism of graded $`R`-algebras
   $$`\operatorname{OS}_R(\mathcal{A})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\bigoplus_{q\ge0}H^{q,q}(U_{\mathcal{A}}).`

The ring $`R` is arbitrary: it can be $`\mathbb{Z}`, $`\mathbb{Z}/2` or the zero ring, $`2` is never assumed to be invertible, and $`B` need not be flat over $`R`.
No generator of any Tate line is chosen, and $`U_{\mathcal{A}}` may have no $`k`-points at all.
This is {bp "thm:affine"}[] together with {bp "cor:ordinary"}[].
:::

For the projective case, let $`\mathcal{P}` be a nonempty arrangement of hyperplanes in $`\mathbb{P}^n_k`, with equations $`\ell_i` and central cone $`c\mathcal{P}`.
All the intersections of the cone are nonempty, so the signed boundary $`\partial` descends to $`\operatorname{OS}_B(c\mathcal{P})`.
Its kernel $`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})` is the subalgebra generated by the differences $`e_i-e_j`.

:::statement "Main theorem, projective case"
With the same theory, for every nonempty arrangement $`\mathcal{P}` in $`\mathbb{P}^n_k`:

 * *The total ring.* There is an isomorphism of $`B`-algebras
   $$`\overline{\Phi}_{\mathcal{P}}:\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_B(\mathcal{P})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C(U_{\mathcal{P}}),\qquad e_i-e_j\longmapsto[\ell_i/\ell_j],`
   which preserves the bigrading. It is defined with no hyperplane at infinity, and it does not depend on the equations.
 * *The diagonal.* There is a canonical isomorphism of graded $`R`-algebras
   $$`\overline{\operatorname{OS}}_R(\mathcal{P})\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\bigoplus_{q\ge0}H^{q,q}(U_{\mathcal{P}}).`

This is {bp "thm:projective"}[], and {ref "projective"}[its chapter] explains the construction.
:::

*The same theorems in Lean.*
The code on this page is elaborated against the library every time the book is built, so it cannot drift from the library.
An `example` asks Lean to check that the term after `:=` has the type stated before it.
The type is the mathematical statement, and the term is the library's declaration.
First come the hypotheses, in which the cohomology theory is the argument `T`.

```lean -show
noncomputable section
open OrlikSolomon OrlikSolomon.SmoothAlg OrlikSolomon.OSAlgebra
open scoped TensorProduct DirectSum
universe u v w z
```

```lean
variable
  -- the base field
  {k : Type u} [Field k]
  -- the coefficient ring
  {R : Type v} [CommRing R]
  -- the cohomology of the point, with its weight pieces
  {B : Type w} [CommRing B] [Algebra R B]
  {ℬ : ℤ → Submodule R B} [GradedAlgebra ℬ]
  -- the cohomology theory: this is the hypothesis
  (T : OrdinaryTheory.{u, v, w, z} k ℬ)
  -- a finite-dimensional space and an arrangement in it
  {V : Type u} [AddCommGroup V] [Module k V]
  [FiniteDimensional k V]
  (𝒜 : Arrangement k V)
```

The total ring, then the two facts that make it graded and canonical.

```lean
example : OSAlgebra B 𝒜 ≃ₐ[B] T.C.obj (complement 𝒜) :=
  T.affineEquiv 𝒜

example :
    IsHomogeneousOfShift (bigrading 𝒜 ℬ)
      (T.grading (complement 𝒜)) 0 (T.affineEquiv 𝒜) :=
  T.isHomogeneousOfShift_affineEquiv 𝒜

example {f : V →ᵃ[k] k} (H : 𝒜) (hf : IsEquation f H) :
    T.affineEquiv 𝒜 (generator B 𝒜 H) =
      T.logClass (complementUnit 𝒜 f (hf.covers H.2)) :=
  T.affineEquiv_generator_of_isEquation H hf
```

The groups, and their vanishing outside the range $`0\le p\le n`.

```lean
example (p : ℕ) (w : ℤ) :
    degree R 𝒜 p ⊗[R] ℬ (w - p) ≃ₗ[R]
      T.grading (complement 𝒜) ((p : ℤ), w) :=
  T.ordinaryComponentEquiv 𝒜 p w

example {p w : ℤ}
    (hp : p < 0 ∨ (Module.finrank k V : ℤ) < p) :
    T.grading (complement 𝒜) (p, w) = ⊥ :=
  T.grading_complement_eq_bot hp
```

The diagonal.

```lean
example :
    OSAlgebra R 𝒜 ≃ₐ[R]
      ⨁ q : ℕ, diagonalGrading
        (T.grading (complement 𝒜)) q :=
  T.diagonalEquiv 𝒜
```

For the projective theorem, the arrangement lives in a vector space $`W` of dimension $`n+1`.
The hypothesis `[Nonempty P]` is what excludes the empty arrangement.

```lean
variable
  {W : Type u} [AddCommGroup W] [Module k W]
  [FiniteDimensional k W]
  (P : ProjectiveArrangement k W) [Nonempty P]

example :
    projectiveAlgebra B P.cone_isCentral ≃ₐ[B]
      T.C.obj (projectiveComplement P) :=
  T.projectiveEquiv P

example :
    projectiveAlgebra R P.cone_isCentral ≃ₐ[R]
      ⨁ q : ℕ, diagonalGrading
        (T.grading (projectiveComplement P)) q :=
  T.projectiveDiagonalEquiv P
```

