These are theorems about one cohomology theory, algebraic de Rham cohomology, over fields of characteristic zero.
The other statements of the main theorem hold for the de Rham value of the structure as well, by instantiation, but they are about its twisted algebra $`H^\bullet_{\mathrm{dR}}(X/k)\otimes_kk[t,t^{-1}]` and say nothing more about de Rham cohomology.

*Other theories.*
For every other cohomology theory the theorems remain conditional.
That the axioms hold for complex singular cohomology has been checked by hand, field by field, and the checks are recorded in the comments of the source.
For geometric $`\ell`-adic cohomology the source records the analogous interpretation, together with what an instance would still have to supply.
These are mathematical arguments and not formal proofs.
So the library does not yet contain a theorem about singular or $`\ell`-adic cohomology.
To obtain one, you must construct a value of the structure, and {bp "lem:standard-bridge"}[the bridge] reduces this to the familiar degreewise inputs: groups, products, pullbacks, localization with supports and oriented purity.
{ref "axioms"}[The chapter on the axioms] explains each field, the checks and the bridge.

The axioms are deliberately much weaker than the conclusion.
No axiom asserts that logarithmic classes exist or generate, that the arrangement relations hold, that residues are surjective, that Gysin maps vanish, that a deletion sequence is short exact, that anything is free, or the structure theorems themselves.
All of these are outputs of the proof.

*What is outside the theorems.*
 * Hyperplanes are defined over $`k`. Nonsplit arrangements are not considered.
 * The dimension axiom says that the point has cohomology only in cohomological degree zero. Arithmetic cohomology over a nonclosed field usually fails it, and so do many generalized theories, so they are outside these theorems. The blueprint's separate additive treatment of such theories by a stable splitting is {ref "generalized"}[deferred]: it is out of scope for now and nothing about it is formalized.
 * In the projective case the empty arrangement is excluded. There is no hyperplane to put at infinity, the space is $`\mathbb{P}^n` itself, and its cohomology needs a projective bundle formula that the axioms do not provide.

*How this book is tied to the code.*
Each blueprint result appears in a box that shows a status and its label in one of four blueprints: the [first](https://github.com/adamtopaz/OrlikSolomon/blob/main/blueprint/arrangement_cohomology_lean_blueprint.tex), for the main theorem, the [second](https://github.com/adamtopaz/OrlikSolomon/blob/main/blueprint/de_rham_instance_blueprint.tex), for the de Rham instance, the [third](https://github.com/adamtopaz/OrlikSolomon/blob/main/blueprint/geometric_refactor_blueprint.tex), for the refactor on schemes, or the [fourth](https://github.com/adamtopaz/OrlikSolomon/blob/main/blueprint/cohomological_hyperplane_gt_blueprint.tex), for the planned cohomological reconstruction application:
 * *Planned* means that the result is in scope and no Lean declaration is attached to it yet. The text describes what is intended, and nothing has been checked.
 * *Deferred* means that the result is in the blueprint but outside the current scope of the project.
 * *Partly formalized* means that the box names Lean declarations for part of the result, shows their statements with links to their source, and says what is missing.
 * *Formalized* means that the box names Lean declarations and shows their statements with links to their source, and that review of the change found that they cover the whole result.

The declarations of a box are collapsed under a line at its foot, so that a chapter reads as mathematics.
Open that line to compare them with the text.

*What the build checks.*
A box cannot claim the last two statuses by assertion, because they follow from naming declarations.
For each named declaration, the build of this book checks only that it exists, and that it depends on no `sorry` and on no axiom other than Lean's standard `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound`.
A second check fails the build if any of the blueprints has a node that the book does not present, if the book presents a node twice, or if a label is used twice, even in two different blueprints.
The {ref "status"}[status page] is generated from the same data.
Lean code in the text, such as the statements above, is elaborated when the book is built.
So are the names of declarations that the text sets in Lean's highlighting, and the build fails if one of them does not resolve.

*What the build does not check.*
The build does not check that the named declarations cover the statement in their box.
It would accept any proved declaration there, even one that has nothing to do with the result.
It also does not check any prose, inside or outside the boxes.
Both of these are checked by human or agent review of each change, not by the build, and review can make mistakes.
So do not read *Formalized* as a machine-checked claim about the words in a box.
What the build has checked is the declarations shown there, and you can compare their statements with the text yourself.

*A de Rham instance.*
The status lines at the top of this page count each blueprint separately.
The second blueprint constructs a value of the structure of axioms: algebraic de Rham cohomology over a field of characteristic zero.
That value is formalized, so the axioms have a nonzero model in Lean.
So are the forms of the two theorems with no hypothetical cohomology theory in them, which instantiate the theorems at this value and identify the generators with the classes of the forms $`df/f`, as stated above.
{ref "de-rham"}[Its chapter] presents the construction and the worked regressions.
The main theorem does not depend on it: the dependence runs the other way.

*The formulation on schemes.*
The third blueprint restates both theorems for cohomology theories defined on smooth schemes, with the complements as actual open subschemes of affine and projective space, so that a theory on all smooth schemes applies to them directly.
It reuses every proved algebraic result through the equivalence between smooth algebras and smooth affine schemes, and transports the de Rham instance to smooth affine schemes.
Its formalized foundations include: admissible domains of smooth schemes, the equivalence between smooth algebras and smooth affine schemes, zero schemes and basic opens, products with the affine line and the multiplicative group, units as maps to the multiplicative group, principal smooth divisor pairs with their affine duality, affine space with arrangement complements, deletion pairs and relation targets as open subschemes, and projective space with projective arrangement complements as its open subschemes.
The geometric cohomology structure and both affine transports are also formalized, with canonical graded comparisons commuting with Gysin and residue.
The supports-and-purity bridge constructs the geometric theory from degreewise cohomology with products, localization and purity on any admissible domain, including non-affine objects, and commutes with restriction and affine transport.
The logarithmic classes of global units and the decomposition of the cohomology of a product with the multiplicative group are formalized on every object of a domain, affine or not.
The sheaves of relative forms now have their differential, wedge product and scheme pullbacks, with the canonical comparison to algebraic forms on affine schemes.
So are both structure theorems on the actual arrangement opens, for a theory on any admissible domain, obtained from the theorems of this page by transport and not by a second proof.
Applying the affine transport to the existing de Rham theory gives a nonzero theory on smooth affine schemes, whose cohomology is the cohomology of global sheaf forms, so the Orlik–Solomon theorems hold unconditionally for the de Rham cohomology of the actual open subschemes.
The logarithmic sheaf residue sequence and its local quasi-isomorphism are proved separately and compared with the affine instance.
Worked geometric regressions include complements without rational points, unit and disconnected pairs, non-flat pullback, residue multiplicity, and the projective line, where global forms differ from its two-chart Čech complex.
{ref "geometric"}[Its chapter] presents it, and its nodes are counted separately above.
The algebraic structure theorems and their algebraic de Rham corollaries do not depend on this refactor.

*A planned reconstruction application.*
The fourth blueprint proposes recovering the Galois group of a perfect field from the marked degree-one and degree-two cohomology diagram of hyperplane complements over its algebraic closure, up to a central scalar subgroup.
The coefficient ring is required to be nonzero and local, or an integral domain, and the ambient dimension is at least two.
The proof first classifies automorphisms of an explicit Orlik–Solomon diagram, then uses the existing structure theorem to pass to a supplied theory satisfying the axioms.
Its characteristic-zero de Rham specialization is also planned; no reconstruction theorem is formalized yet.
The blueprint has 38 named groups containing 41 separately labeled statements: thirty-three are formalized and eight remain planned in {ref "hyperplane-gt"}[the new chapter], counted separately above.
The completed statements cover G01–G06, A01–A04, C01–C03, U01–U05, L01–L05, P01–P02, I01–I04 and S02, including the actual universal configuration equations and paired centralizer invariance in U04.
A04 compares the actual cohomology diagrams with their presentations for a supplied full theory; it constructs no new nonzero theory instance.
L04 uses only unit cancellation over a commutative ring; L05 detects a unique graph delta over a nonzero local ring, using the actual centralizer restriction and the explicit dimension bound.

